  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Thursday...\n\nIsolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms are forecast \ntoday. This morning, a few showers, and maybe weak thunderstorms \n(up to 15 percent chance), are forecast over south- eastern \nMontana before additional isolated to scattered showers and \nthunderstorms develop across the area this afternoon and evening. \nWith modest instability (CAPE values around 500 to 1500 J/kg) and \nshear (deep layer shear around 20 to 35 knots), a few \nthunderstorms may become strong to severe over eastern Montana \nduring the afternoon and evening. The one potentially limiting \nfactor is the modest shear which may not be enough to allow \nthunderstorms to organize and become long-lived, causing \nthunderstorms to develop and decay relatively quickly. With that \nsaid, the risk is still there for severe thunderstorms and the \nStorm Prediction Center has a Marginal Risk (1/5) of severe \nthunderstorms over far eastern Montana, east of Billings. The main\nthreats with any severe thunderstorm that develops today are \nhail, strong winds, and heavy rain. While severe thunderstorms are\nnot expected over south-central Montana, a strong thunderstorm \ncould (moderate chance) still develop in this area and produce \nbrief gusty winds, small hail, and heavy rain. \n\nSaturday, the 4th of July, is forecast to be a hot and mostly dry\nday with high temperatures in the 80s to lower 90s. While the \nmajority of the area is expected to remain dry, there is a low \nchance (15 percent) that showers linger from Friday into early \nSaturday morning over far south- eastern Montana. \n\nSunday will be a hot day with high temperatures in the mid 80s to\nupper 90s. There is even a modest, 40 percent chance of seeing a \nfew 100F readings along the river valleys of eastern Montana. \nDuring the afternoon and evening, a low to moderate chance of \nshowers and thunderstorms returns as a trough approaches the \nregion to the northwest. With this, the chance of seeing a severe \nthunderstorm is low. \n\nMonday through Thursday, hot conditions will continue with highs \ngenerally in the 80s and 90s. Monday looks to be the \"coolest\" day\nbehind a backdoor cold front. Along with the heat, a daily chance\nof showers and thunderstorms continues through the week as \nmoisture moving in under the upper level ridge combines with a few\nweak shortwaves rounding the ridge. With that said, will have to \nwatch how warm temperatures get aloft as this can inhibit \nconvection in our region, keeping a mid-level cap in place. LA",
